 "coaching": {
  "headline": "0 kills to 10 by the surrender — the game was decided in our jungle before the seven-minute mark.",
  "team": "Our jungle gave up six deaths before minute seven (five of them solo catches), Drongo and Kallari snowballed off it, and the only two fights on the film (7.1 at their Fangtooth, 9.4 at their tower) were lost with our winning lanes flagged as never joining. The draft note matters too: our damage was 4/5 magical — one enemy magic-resist item blunts the whole comp — and nobody completed an item before the end. When a jungle collapses that fast, the duo play is to shrink the map: ward the river entrances, concede the contested farm, and force the snowball through a warded choke instead of feeding it clean angles.",
  "whatShiftedIt": "Six pre-7-minute deaths in our jungle bought their Fangtooth at 7.1, and the 9.4 fight (1-3 at their tower) ended any way back before the surrender.",
  "perPlayer": {
   "a0c393cc-1437-4bdc-8168-64676ce2166c": "Sparrow's lane read Drongo's and the film agrees (0/1/2 against a 6/1 Drongo) — an 11-minute game gives a losing carry no levers. The one available move: with 4 wards placed, Sparrow was the duo's only vision source; against a fed Kallari, river wards from minute two are the difference between a bad lane and a 0-10 board.",
   "10edbf65-457d-48d0-9151-9df5b4fcb40a": "Gadget won the mid matchup on paper all game and still went 0/3 — the deaths were map deaths, including the 4.3 catch by Kallari, and the 9.4 tower fight lost 1-3. With one ward placed, mid had no view of a jungler who finished 4/1. The read from a winning lane in a collapsing game: ward first, then shove-and-rotate only through vision."
  }
 },
